<zk>
	<style>
		.qa-sclass-probe { background-color: rgb(200, 12, 53) !important; }
	</style>
	<label multiline="true">
		Both boxes below declare sclass="qa-sclass-probe", and the style rule above paints
		any element carrying that class dark red.

		- Expected: BOTH boxes are dark red — each root renders the user sclass alongside
			its zclass (e.g. class="qa-sclass-probe z-daterangebox").
		- Bug: only the datebox is red; the daterangebox root renders class="z-daterangebox"
			without the user sclass, so the sclass-scoped CSS never applies.

		The buttons cover the runtime paths: a setSclass must swap the custom class and a
		setSclass(null) must remove it, and the zclass must survive both.
	</label>
	<!-- State modifiers must survive a setSclass too: a setSclass rewrites
		className wholesale, so anything only ever added with classList is lost
		unless domClass_() re-emits it. "readonly" then "change sclass" must leave
		the box both z-daterangebox-readonly and qa-sclass-updated; typing garbage
		in the begin input then "change sclass" must keep z-daterangebox-invalid.
		The buttons sit above the boxes because an errorbox opens to its input's
		right, where it would cover them. -->
	<div>
		<button id="btnChange" label="change sclass"
			onClick='drb.setSclass("qa-sclass-updated")'/>
		<button id="btnClear" label="clear sclass"
			onClick='drb.setSclass(null)'/>
		<button id="btnReadonly" label="readonly"
			onClick='drb.setReadonly(true)'/>
	</div>
	<div>
		<datebox id="db" sclass="qa-sclass-probe"/>
	</div>
	<div>
		<daterangebox id="drb" sclass="qa-sclass-probe"/>
	</div>
</zk>
